Now I understand the issue. On Safari, `asyncOpenURL` opens a blank window synchronously (`window.open()`) to avoid pop-up blocking. Then sets `windowRef.location` when the promise resolves. 

In `ExitSurveyConfirmPage.tsx`, the `onPress` first calls `ExitSurvey.switchToOldDot().then(() => { ... Link.openOldDotLink(...) })`. The `Link.openOldDotLink` is called inside the `.then()` callback, which is asynchronous relative to the original user gesture. By the time `asyncOpenURL` calls `window.open()`, Safari no longer considers it a user-initiated event, so `window.open()` returns null (popup blocked). Therefore `windowRef` is null, and nothing happens.

The fix would be to not call `Link.openOldDotLink` inside the promise callback but rather call it synchronously in onPress. Or restructure so the window.open happens synchronously in the click handler.

